1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,833
…If you buy a McDonald's franchise, you get a two hundred page disclosure document.

2
00:00:04,833 --> 00:00:10,399
Federal law requires it. It has the pricing, the exit terms, what happens if McDonald's

3
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:13,433
changes the menu and your numbers stop working.

4
00:00:13,433 --> 00:00:17,133
Three hundred eighty five thousand people bought a Helium hotspot.

5
00:00:17,133 --> 00:00:20,299
For their franchise disclosure equivalent, they just got vibes

6
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:25,433
Helium operators bought a franchise, but they were sold a business.

7
00:00:25,433 --> 00:00:31,833
The hardware they paid for is theirs. The pricing that determines what the hardware earns is set by Nova Labs.

8
00:00:31,833 --> 00:00:37,999
Nova Labs is the company that runs Helium Mobile and negotiates the carrier deals with T-Mobile and AT&T.

9
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,733
That's the whole piece.
The rest is just verification…
 
A pro outdoor helium hot spot is $949.

10
00:00:45,733 --> 00:00:52,066
After the August 2025 halving, a well placed urban unit earns four to eight dollars a month.

11
00:00:52,066 --> 00:00:55,199
At eight dollars, the hardware pays itself off in ten years.

12
00:00:55,200 --> 00:01:00,400
At four, twenty. Ten years is what asphalt shingles take to wear out.

13
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:06,400
Twenty is most of a mortgage. The whole IoT side of the network, all three hundred eighty five

14
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:11,966
thousand hotspots, generates $125 a day in data transfer revenue.

15
00:01:11,966 --> 00:01:15,066
The mobile side, which is the carrier offload for T-Mobile and

16
00:01:15,066 --> 00:01:19,099
AT&T, generates $56,000 a day.

17
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:22,200
The hotspot side is the hardware the project points at.

18
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:28,900
The mobile side is where the revenue actually lives
 
In April 2025, a governance vote

19
00:01:28,900 --> 00:01:32,333
called HIP-143 gave Nova Labs the authority to

20
00:01:32,333 --> 00:01:36,299
negotiate carrier pricing without routing each deal through governance.

21
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:42,400
Now, carrier deals are confidential, and governance is slow, so the commercial case is valid.

22
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:46,700
The proposal was sold as a one year sunset. But read the text closer.

23
00:01:46,700 --> 00:01:51,033
It extends for another year if no overriding HIP passes.

24
00:01:51,033 --> 00:01:56,299
April 2026, the initial year ended. No overriding HIP passed.

25
00:01:56,300 --> 00:02:00,566
The authority extended itself. It now runs through April 2027.

26
00:02:00,566 --> 00:02:06,166
Nobody overrode it
 
HIP-143 passed at 90%.

27
00:02:06,166 --> 00:02:09,766
NOVA Labs voted yes with 26% of the total vote.

28
00:02:09,766 --> 00:02:16,266
A proxy named ferebee, one of the four coauthors who wrote the proposal, voted yes with another 24%.

29
00:02:16,266 --> 00:02:22,799
Together, half the yes votes authorizing Nova Labs pricing authority came from Nova Labs and one of its coauthors.

30
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:28,633
Six months later, a vote called HIP-148 consolidated more rewards to Nova Labs.

31
00:02:28,633 --> 00:02:34,599
Subscribers who had been earning Helium tokens for sharing location data from their phones got gift cards instead.

32
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:40,200
That vote passed at 97%. The top three voters controlled 62% of it.

33
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:44,400
In those six months, ferebee's voting stake grew 55%.

34
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:47,566
The rest of the network grew 18%.

35
00:02:47,566 --> 00:02:54,266
Every HIP starts from a more concentrated baseline than the last
 
I ran twelve Datagram nodes.

36
00:02:54,266 --> 00:03:00,299
I moderated their community for seven months. I did it without pay, and the project collapsed.

37
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:03,700
Earlier pieces I published called Datagram the legitimate counterexample

38
00:03:03,700 --> 00:03:07,366
the decentralized infrastructure project doing it right.

39
00:03:07,366 --> 00:03:12,166
That framing was wrong.
The four disclosure standard this piece argues for would have caught

40
00:03:12,166 --> 00:03:15,666
Helium in 2021 before any hotspot shipped.

41
00:03:15,666 --> 00:03:19,699
It would have flagged that pricing authority sat outside the operator class.

42
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:23,900
The same standard would not have caught Datagram. Those documents passed.

43
00:03:23,900 --> 00:03:28,033
The software wasn't running what they described.
The audit is the floor.

44
00:03:28,033 --> 00:03:34,466
It doesn't catch fraud
 
The piece on Substack covers what this video skipped.

45
00:03:34,466 --> 00:03:38,966
the four disclosures Helium operators never received, written out in plain language: a

46
00:03:38,966 --> 00:03:43,532
hardware buyback floor an exit provision when the reward structure changes underneath you

47
00:03:43,533 --> 00:03:48,599
revenue disclosure, even when individual carrier rates stay confidential a sunset clause that

48
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:55,366
requires an active revote instead of extending by default
Also, the full HIP-148 vote, the

49
00:03:55,366 --> 00:03:59,966
mobile mapping rewards that subscribers earned by sharing location data, traded for redeemable

50
00:03:59,966 --> 00:04:05,566
gift cards, and the Helium Foundation's own guidance, pointing operators toward the same two

51
00:04:05,566 --> 00:04:08,999
proxies that were accumulating the most voting power.

52
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,400
And finally, the rationalization that made me run twelve datagram nodes

53
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:16,366
without pay for a project that had no business underneath it.

